{"name":"Cookie Dough","hex":"#ab7100","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cookie dough","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A paste usually formed of flour, sugar, water, eggs, and other ingredients which, when baked, hardens to form a cookie or a number of cookies.","A flavor designed to emulate the taste of the paste."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cookie_dough"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cookie dough","blocks":[{"text":"Cookie dough is an uncooked blend of cookie ingredients. While cookie dough is typically meant to be baked into individual cookies before consumption, edible cookie dough is specifically designed to be eaten raw; it is usually made without eggs and contains heat-treated flour to ensure safety for human consumption."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Cookiedough.jpg/330px-Cookiedough.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cookie_dough","wikidata":"Q5167099"},{"title":"Cookie","blocks":[{"text":"A cookie is a sweet biscuit with high sugar and fat content. Cookie dough is softer than that used for other types of biscuit, and they are cooked longer at lower temperatures. The dough typically contains flour, sugar, egg, and some type of oil or fat. It may include other ingredients such as raisins, oats, chocolate chips, or nuts. Cookie texture varies from crisp and crunchy to soft and chewy, depending on the exact combination of ingredients and methods used to create them."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/2ChocolateChipCookies.jpg/330px-2ChocolateChipCookies.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cookie","wikidata":"Q13266"},{"title":"Dough","blocks":[{"text":"Dough is a malleable, sometimes elastic paste most commonly made from flour or meal. Dough is typically made by mixing flour with a small amount of water or other liquid, and sometimes includes yeast, other leavening agents, or ingredients such as fats or flavourings."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Dough.jpg/330px-Dough.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dough","wikidata":"Q178024"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/R%C5%AFzn%C3%A9_druhy_cukrov%C3%AD_%282%29.jpg/500px-R%C5%AFzn%C3%A9_druhy_cukrov%C3%AD_%282%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/R%C5%AFzn%C3%A9_druhy_cukrov%C3%AD_%282%29.jpg/1280px-R%C5%AFzn%C3%A9_druhy_cukrov%C3%AD_%282%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/R%C5%AFzn%C3%A9_druhy_cukrov%C3%AD_%282%29.jpg/500px-R%C5%AFzn%C3%A9_druhy_cukrov%C3%AD_%282%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A dish of assorted cookies, including sandwich cookies filled with jam","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AR%C5%AFzn%C3%A9_druhy_cukrov%C3%AD_(2).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Biscits_or_cookiess.jpg/500px-Biscits_or_cookiess.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Biscits_or_cookiess.jpg/1280px-Biscits_or_cookiess.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Biscits_or_cookiess.jpg/500px-Biscits_or_cookiess.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cookies baking in an oven","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABiscits_or_cookiess.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Holiday_C